	<h2>
		Summary
	</h2>
	<p>
		Since its first running in 1979, the Dakar has been regarded as the toughest motorsport
		event on the planet. Every year in the first week of January, hundreds of off-road
		enthusiasts set out with their works and privateer cars on a journey through one of
		the most inhospitable regions on earth - the Sahara. Stages of up to 500 km per day
		are covered, so that over the 14 days this event lasts, more than 2000 km quickly
		add up. Traditionally there is a one-day break in the middle of the rally, which is
		used for repairs and general recovery.
	</p>
	<figure>
		<img src="~/images/dakar/Dakar2007.jpg" alt="The Dakar 2007 logo" />
		<figcaption>The 2007 Dakar logo</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<h2>
		History
	</h2>
	<p>
		The rally was initiated and conceived by the French motorsportsman Thierry Sabine
		and was first held in 1978. Sabine had been inspired by his participation in the
		Abidjan-Nice rally, during which he got lost with his motorcycle in the Libyan
		desert in 1977. Sabine's much-quoted motto &ldquo;If life gets boring,
		risk it!&rdquo; found its fulfilment when he himself was killed on 14 January 1986
		in a helicopter crash caused by a sandstorm during the Paris-Dakar Rally. In his
		memory, a hill covered with a small tree and some shrubbery was named
		Arbre Thierry Sabine. A memorial stone and an illustrated book kept there
		commemorate the accident (see web link below). Due to the large number of deaths
		among racers, spectators and the organisation team, the race repeatedly made
		headlines, but nevertheless (or perhaps for that very reason) developed into one
		of the best-known sporting events worldwide. Since its inception, the rally has
		claimed 51 human lives (as of 2006). Most recently, at the 2006 Dakar, the Australian
		motorcycle rider Andy Caldecott and two young spectators died. As a result, timekeeping
		was suspended on the final day of racing in 2006.
	</p>
